#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079356} :activity/id #uuid "1bf76cea-4646-53df-8ca6-de484707e26b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050157 :topic/id #uuid "32ad96da-6870-504d-ad58-7d14df8bea1a" :topic/math-academy-id 668} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079356 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079727 :tutorial/content "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term and $d$ is a common difference.\n\nTo illustrate how this works, suppose we want to find the $20$th term of the following arithmetic sequence:\n${\\color{blue}2}, \\: 5, \\: 8, \\: 11, \\: 14, \\: \\ldots$\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\nd=5-2={\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nSo, we can write the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\n&= 2 + (n - 1)(3) \\\\\n&= 2 + 3n - 3 \\\\\n&= 3n - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $20$th term, we can use this formula with $n=20$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 3n - 1 \\\\\na_{20} &= 3(20) - 1 \\\\\n&= 60 - 1 \\\\\n&= 59\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $20$th term is $a_{20}=59$. Using the formula was much quicker than writing out all $20$ terms of the sequence!" :tutorial/id #uuid "8a96e03d-29c4-5df5-9a65-7ba58b42a896" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8909 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cf2f182d-a477-54e3-bd8c-09edda20c9b2" :step/math-academy-id 187824 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079357}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078463 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078181 :question/id #uuid "3efc515d-87bd-5e90-b2ea-4bdf954302e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-14382"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "952a09bc-d1fa-5e2d-b146-ec28e1c960a9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049579} {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218605057